                        "language": "english",
                        "review": "This review is spoiler-free and doesn't discuss any particular puzzles or scenes.\n\nSo far I'm about two hours into the game, and judging by the number of unlocked achievements and score I've reached the halfway point. Of course, the game will likely ramp up the difficulty later on, so I don't expect to be done in just another two hours.\n\nIt's been a very entertaining experience until now. This is a pure text adventure with real, honest-to-goodness puzzles in it. It's not an interactive story with just some minor decisions points, nor is the focus on storytelling. If you've played earlier Legend Entertainment titles, like Spellcasting 101, TimeQuest, or Eric the Unready, you'll have a good idea what to expect.\n\nThe descriptions are well-written and interspersed with humor that doesn't go over the top with breaking the fourth wall. There are some references to past works of Bob Bates. I particularly enjoyed talking to a certain Mr. Vettenmyer.\n\nThe puzzles have been logical so far, and the way they are designed invites the player to experiment. I don't think there are any dead ends. I managed to die at one point, but the game gave me ample warning beforehand. There is a hint system in the game, but I didn't have to use it yet, as I never got stuck for more than a couple of minutes. The world gradually opens up and doesn't overwhelm the player with a lot of new locations at once.\n\nThe (typing) interface uses standards that were establish during the Infocom days, with handy abbreviations like \"x\" for examine and \"g\" for repeating the previous command. Moving around can become a bit cumbersome if I need to go from one location in Manhattan to another. As far as I know, there is no fast-travel option, probably because skipping rooms could circumvent events that are supposed to happen if the player passes through.\n\nNaturally, there are no graphics, except for a diagram that shows the current room layout and a (weirdly stretched) map of Manhattan. Sound is completely absent as well, but if I wanted to, I could just start a music player in the background.\n\nAll in all, this has been a pleasant two hours. The central game mechanic (which I won't spoil here) offers interesting ways of interacting with objects and people. At one point, I found a method to learn details about the NPCs, which added more depth to their personalities. That was a nice touch.\n\nIf you are looking for an old-school adventure game that harkens back to the days of Infocom and Legend, Thaumistry gets my recommendation.",
